===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# To a [[great]] [[extent]] or [[degree]].
#: {{syn|en|greatly|drastically|extremely}}
#: {{ux|en|That dress is '''very''' you.}}
#: {{ux|en|Not '''very''' many (of them) had been damaged.}}
#: {{ux|en|She's '''very''' similar to her mother.}}
#: {{ux|en|‘Is she busy?’ ― ‘Not '''very'''.’}}
#* {{RQ:Belloc Lowndes Lodger|II|0091
|passage=Then his sallow face brightened, for the hall had been carefully furnished, and was '''very''' clean. <br> There was a neat hat-and-umbrella stand, and the stranger's weary feet fell soft on a good, serviceable dark-red drugget, which matched in colour the flock-paper on the walls.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=13|passage=“{{...}}&nbsp;They talk of you as if you were Croesus—and I expect the beggars sponge on you unconscionably.” And Vickers launched forth into a tirade '''very''' different from his platform utterances. He spoke with extreme contempt of the dense stupidity exhibited on all occasions by the working classes.}}
# Conforming to [[fact]], [[reality]] or [[rule]]; [[true]].
#: {{syn|en|truly|actually|authentically}}
# {{lb|en|with superlatives}} {{ngd|Used to firmly establish that nothing else surpasses in some respect.}}
#: {{ux|en|He was the '''very''' best runner there.}}
#: {{ux|en|This is my '''very''' own treehouse.}}

====Usage notes====
* When used in their senses as degree adverbs, "very" and "[[too]]" never modify verbs (except in some dialects influenced by Chinese: see [[Citations:very|citations]]).

